  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chances of showers and thunderstorms continue \nthrough Sunday. In addition, heat and humidity will persist through \nthis weekend and into early next week. \n\nEarly morning weather analysis shows weak SW flow aloft, while at \nthe surface high pressure offshore continues to remain in control. \nGOES Sat imagery shows mostly clear skies across the area this \nmorning. As for temperatures, low to middle 70s have been noted \nalong and west of I-95, while to the east temperatures continue to \nremain in the upper 70s to low 80s (mainly along the coast). Even \nthough skies are clear temperatures are not expected to drop much \nfurther (perhaps a degree or two) due to the high pressure offshore \ncontinuing to fetch in warm and humid air. Through the day a normal \nsummer time pattern will be in place with scattered cumulus clouds \nin the afternoon and temperatures  reaching into the low 90s. Dew \npoints will once again struggle to mix out through the day and \nremain in the low to middle 70s. The mix of heat and humidity will \nallow Heat Indices to be in the upper 90s and low 100s, but will \nremain shy of the 105F Heat Advisory Criteria. In terms of storm \nchances and coverage the highest confidence in storms is along and \nnorth of I-64. This is due to where the greatest forcing is expected \nas a weak 700mb short wave moves through the area as well as this \narea is expected to hit ConvT. At this time, there is low confidence \non severe storms. However, due to the hot and humid airmass in place \na very isolated strong to severe storm cannot be ruled out. For \nSunday, the weather pattern will remain quite similar with the \nchance of showers and thunderstorms along the Lee Trough, high \nterrain, and Sea Breeze in the afternoon. In addition to the showers \nand thunderstorms, the heat and humidity will persist with temps \nranging in the low to middle 90s and Heat Indices between the upper \n90s and low 100s but again remaining shy of the 105F Heat Advisory \ncriteria. \n\nBy early next week, ensembles continue to hint on a more zonal \npattern across the area with the strongest flow remaining north of \nthe area. While at the surface high pressure continues to thrive off \nthe coast continuing to usher in heat and moisture. During the early \nto mid-week stretch the chances of showers and storms persist with \nthe best chance  now possibly being late Tuesday into early \nWednesday morning as a stronger cold front tries to move into the \narea. Outside of the daily chances of showers and storms, \ntemperatures are expected to rise above normal with temperatures \npossibly reaching into the lower 90s. With dew points struggling to \nmix through the day as the high is offshore they will remain the low \nto middle 70s again allowing Heat Indices to be in the low 100s. \nWill note there is a possibility that some places could reach Heat \nAdvisory criteria Monday through Wednesday but confidence at this \ntime remains low.",
